  "course_description": "Variety of subjects pertaining to religion. Content will vary in successive offerings. May be repeated for credit when topic varies.",
  "course_notes": "(Same as ANT 285 2, LACS 270 3, HIST 296 3, SOC 289 2) This course satisfies the Religion major requirement:- Tradition-Christianity, Region. Throughout Latin American history, men and women have repeatedly proclaimed the arrival of a new age. Some predicted the end of the world, others the return of ancestral gods, the coming of a messiah, or the creation of a just society free from oppression. Their movements inspired hope, challenged political authority, mobilized thousands of followers, and sometimes sparked violent rebellions. This course examines the history of prophetic, millenarian, and messianic movements from the colonial period to the twentieth century. Through case studies ranging from the Taki Onqoy movement in the Andes and the Pueblo Revolt to Canudos, the Talking Cross of Yucatán, the Contestado rebellion, and the Cristero uprising, we will explore how religious visions became powerful vehicles for social protest, political resistance, and collective action. Particular attention will be paid to charismatic leadership, prophecy, persuasion, race, colonialism, state formation, and competing visions of justice, redemption, and social transformation",
